        <strong>Does Ruby lose colour over time?</strong>
        <p>No. Natural Ruby retains its colour permanently. Fading is almost always a sign of glass filling or poor treatment — not the stone itself.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'yellow-sapphire' -%}
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Why do some Yellow Sapphires lose colour?</strong>
        <p>Usually due to unstable natural colour centres or irradiation treatment that fades over time. Always ask specifically for colour-stability assurance — it is non-negotiable for astrological use.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Are your Yellow Sapphires treated?</strong>
        <p>We carry only natural Yellow Sapphires that are either untreated or heat-treated, with full disclosure on every certification. No diffusion-treated or glass-filled stones, ever.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Which origin is best for astrology?</strong>
        <p>Untreated stones from Sri Lanka, Burma, and Madagascar are generally the most trusted for astrological purposes. Sri Lanka remains the classic benchmark.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>What is the correct weight?</strong>
        <p>Traditionally 2–7 carats (approximately 2.25–8 ratti). Quality, clarity, and colour stability matter more than hitting a specific weight.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>How soon do benefits appear?</strong>
        <p>Typically within 4–6 weeks, depending on Jupiter's placement and the strength of its influence in the wearer's chart.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Can it be worn daily?</strong>
        <p>Yes. At Mohs 9, Yellow Sapphire is highly durable for daily wear when securely set.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Which metal pairs best?</strong>
        <p>Gold, traditionally. Panchdhatu may also be used when advised.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>Can Yellow Sapphire be worn with other gemstones?</strong>
        <p>Yes — but compatibility depends entirely on the individual horoscope and should always be confirmed through proper astrological guidance before combining stones.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'white-sapphire' -%}
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Is White Sapphire a replacement for Diamond?</strong>
        <p>Yes, in Vedic astrology, White Sapphire is the primary and most effective "Upratana" or alternative to Diamond.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Does it look like a Diamond?</strong>
        <p>It has high brilliance but lacks the specific "fire" or rainbows of a diamond, appearing more composed and white.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Which origin is best?</strong>
        <p>Sri Lankan (Ceylon) White Sapphires are the world's finest for their lack of yellow or grey undertones.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>Can it be worn on the ring finger?</strong>
        <p>Yes, it is often worn on the ring or middle finger for Venus remedies.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'red-coral' -%}
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Is Red Coral a gemstone or an organic material?</strong>
        <p>It is an organic gemstone, formed from marine life rather than mineral deposits — which is what gives it its distinct character, care requirements, and astrological significance.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Does Red Coral wear out with use?</strong>
        <p>Mild surface wear is natural over time and does not reduce astrological effectiveness. Professional repolishing can restore the surface finish when needed.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Is fake Coral common in the market?</strong>
        <p>Yes — more so than almost any other gemstone. Dyed, plastic, ceramic, resin, and reconstructed coral are widely sold. Always insist on independent certification before purchasing.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Which Coral is best for astrology?</strong>
        <p>Italian (Sardinian) Red Coral is the traditional benchmark. Japanese (Aka) Red Coral is the refined premium choice. Both are natural, untreated, and well-suited for Mangal remedies.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Can Red Coral be worn daily?</strong>
        <p>Yes, with mindful care. Rotating wear helps preserve the surface polish, and removing it before physical activity extends its longevity significantly.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>Which metal is recommended?</strong>
        <p>Gold or Copper traditionally. Silver only when astrologically advised.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'pearl' -%}
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>What is a "Basra" Pearl?</strong>
        <p>Basra pearls are natural, wild pearls from the Persian Gulf. They are extremely rare, historic, and highly sought after for astrology.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Do you sell real pearls?</strong>
        <p>Yes. We provide Natural, South Sea, and high-quality Freshwater pearls, all certified for authenticity.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Why did my pearl turn yellow?</strong>
        <p>Pearls are organic and can absorb sweat or oils over years. Regular wiping after wear helps maintain their lustre.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>Which metal is best for Pearl?</strong>
        <p>Silver is the best metal for Pearl to enhance its cooling and calming properties.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'hessonite' -%}
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>What exactly is Hessonite?</strong>
        <p>Hessonite is a variety of grossular garnet — a natural crystalline gemstone, not an organic material like Pearl or Coral — traditionally worn as a Vedic remedy for ☊ Rahu.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Is Hessonite always brown?</strong>
        <p>No. Natural Hessonite ranges from honey and amber through cinnamon to cognac. Very dark or muddy stones are typically lower quality. The finest stones have a warm, earthy glow that feels alive rather than flat.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Is Hessonite usually treated?</strong>
        <p>Most quality Hessonite is untreated. Light heat treatment may occur and must always be disclosed. We carry only stones with full treatment transparency.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Can it be worn daily?</strong>
        <p>Yes. Hessonite is durable enough for regular daily wear when properly set and cared for.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>How soon do effects show?</strong>
        <p>Typically within 30–60 days, depending on Rahu's placement and the specifics of the individual horoscope.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>Which metal should it be set in?</strong>
        <p>Silver is traditionally preferred. Gold may be used when astrologically advised.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'cats-eye' -%}
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>What is the difference between Quartz and Chrysoberyl Cat's Eye?</strong>
        <p>Chrysoberyl is much rarer and harder. For Ketu remedies, Chrysoberyl is the primary and most powerful stone.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>What determines the quality of the "eye"?</strong>
        <p>A sharp, straight, and mobile line (chatoyancy) that opens and closes is the sign of a top-quality Lehsunia.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Can Cat's Eye be worn long-term?</strong>
        <p>Usually, it is worn for specific remedial periods or durante Ketu dasha. Regular evaluation by an astrologer is advised.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>Which metal is best?</strong>
        <p>Silver is traditionally preferred for Cat's Eye.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'amethyst' -%}
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>How is Amethyst related to Neelam?</strong>
        <p>Amethyst (Jamuniya) is the primary "Upratana" for Blue Sapphire, offering similar Saturn energy but with much less intensity.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Does Amethyst fade?</strong>
        <p>Yes, long exposure to direct, intense sunlight can cause the purple colour to fade over time. Store in a cool, shaded place.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>Can I wear it for protection?</strong>
        <p>Yes, it is widely used as a protective stone against negative habits and for mental sobriety.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'citrine' -%}
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Is Citrine a replacement for Pukhraj?</strong>
        <p>Yes, Citrine (Sunela) is a popular and effective alternative to Yellow Sapphire for Jupiter-related benefits.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Is natural Citrine rare?</strong>
        <p>Yes, most citrine in the market is heat-treated amethyst. We provide natural, earth-mined citrine for astrological use.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>Which metal is best?</strong>
        <p>Silver or Gold can both be used for Citrine depending on the desired intensity of the remedy.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'turquoise' -%}
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Is Turquoise (Firoza) suitable for daily wear?</strong>
        <p>It is porous and sensitive. It should be worn carefully and kept away from oils, lotions, and soaps.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>What is the "matrix" in Turquoise?</strong>
        <p>The matrix is the webbing of brown or black host rock. While some prefer clean blue, "Spiderweb" matrix is also highly valued.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>Which origin is most famous?</strong>
        <p>Nishapur (Iran) and Sleeping Beauty (Arizona) are the most historic and prized origins for Turquoise.</p>
      </div>
  {%- endcase -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

